  "answer_text": "When people talk about \"clean\" gold, they are referring to **fine gold** (or 24-karat gold). While you will often see it stamped as **99.9%** (three nines) or **99.99%** (four nines) pure, you will never see a stamp for 100% purity. \n\nAchieving absolute, 100% purity is scientifically, chemically, and practically impossible. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Laws of Physics and Thermodynamics (Entropy)\nAt a fundamental level, the universe resists absolute purity. According to the **Second Law of Thermodynamics**, physical systems naturally progress toward a state of disorder (entropy). \nTo have a 100% pure block of gold, you would need trillions of trillions of gold atoms aligned perfectly with absolutely zero foreign atoms. In nature, elements want to mix. At a microscopic level, gold will always attract, bond with, or trap trace amounts of other elements.\n\n### 2. The Limits of Chemical Refining\nGold is refined using aggressive chemical and electrochemical processes. \n* First, the **Miller Process** (bubbling chlorine gas through molten gold) refines gold up to about **99.5%** purity by turning impurities into salts that float to the top. \n* To get cleaner gold, refineries use the **Wohlwill Process**, an electrolytic method that dissolves the gold and uses electricity to redeposit it onto a cathode, leaving impurities behind.\n\nWhile this can get gold to **99.9%** or even **99.99%** purity, it faces the law of **diminishing returns**. As the concentration of impurities drops, it becomes exponentially harder for the chemical processes to \"find\" and isolate the remaining trace atoms of silver, copper, or iron. Attempting to filter out those last few stray atoms is virtually impossible.\n\n### 3. Environmental Contamination during Processing\nEven if a refinery could somehow isolate 100% pure gold in a perfect vacuum, the gold has to be melted, poured, and stamped into bars or coins. \nThe moment the molten gold touches a melting crucible, is exposed to the air, or is poured into a mold, it inevitably absorbs microscopic contaminants. Tiny particles of carbon from the crucible, oxygen from the air, or dust in the room will instantly dissolve into the gold, dropping its purity back below 100%.\n\n### 4. Limits of Scientific Measurement\nTo legally stamp a gold bar as \"100% pure,\" a laboratory would have to scientifically prove that there is not a single foreign atom in the entire bar. \nHowever, even the most advanced assaying equipment\u2014like mass spectrometers\u2014has limits. These machines can only detect impurities down to a certain threshold (parts per million or parts per billion). Because scientists cannot measure down to \"zero\" impurities with absolute certainty, they cannot ethically or legally certify a metal as 100.00% pure. \n\n### 5. Diminishing Economic Returns\nRefining gold from 99.9% to 99.99% is already highly complex and expensive. Trying to squeeze out that final 0.01% or 0.1% would require highly specialized laboratory environments, cost millions of dollars, and yield almost no extra financial value. \n\nBecause **99.9%** (or 99.99%) is already chemically identical to \"pure\" gold for all practical, industrial, and investment purposes, the global gold market accepts this as the gold standard for purity.",
